comments[8]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-05T05:09:34.957Z",maintainer-agent,"Audit 2026-07-05 (second pass) surfaced two mapping conflicts the accepted ADR must fix: (1) restore is claimed by BOTH the history cluster (pm-tqel puts it under pm history subcommands) and the epic target surface (close/close-many/delete/restore) — pick one home; the bulk-lifecycle task pm-ik19 implements whichever placement is accepted. (2) eval is claimed by BOTH pm-6apl (ops cluster: stats/health/validate/gc/telemetry/eval) and the epic target surface (search (eval)) — pick one. Also: three clusters named in the target surface previously had no implementing child; now filed as pm-yql1 (item annotation facets), pm-ik19 (bulk/destructive lifecycle variants), pm-kcs4 (context/next/focus navigation noun)."
  "2026-07-05T06:31:25.238Z",claude-code-agent,"Review response: resolved the two mapping conflicts recorded earlier for the proposed grammar. Restore belongs under the history noun because it replays audited history state; eval belongs under ops because it is a diagnostics/evaluation surface. pm-n7rr and pm-6apl were updated to match this target surface."
  "2026-07-06T08:04:51.336Z",maintainer-agent,"Audit #7 mapping addendum (proposed for acceptance with the ADR): (1) 'aggregate' joins the query-read noun as a pm list projection (e.g. pm list --aggregate <spec>) rather than a top-level command — it is a projection over the same filter surface, matching rule 3. (2) 'test-all' becomes an ops verb (pm ops test-all or pm ops test --all) since it is a workspace-wide diagnostic run, not an item facet; the per-item 'test' facet stays with the annotation-facet grammar (pm-yql1). (3) Package-owned top-level commands (beads, changelog) are explicitly OUT of the core noun budget — they already conform to rule 4 and register via the extension command surface. With these three, all 64 live top-level spellings (measured 2026-07-06) have exactly one home in the target grammar."
  "2026-07-06T22:23:28.631Z",codex,"Grammar audit addendum 2026-07-06/07 codex: the accepted grammar should explicitly classify internal/plumbing commands separately from core nouns and package-owned sugar. test-runs-worker is currently visible in default help/contracts despite being described as internal; pm-lp4j tracks hiding it from default discovery while preserving explicit internal orchestration access."
  "2026-07-06T23:10:38.754Z",maintainer-agent,"Brainstorm addition for the accepted revision (2026-07-07): add a principle 7 — SHARED VERB VOCABULARY. Every noun uses the same small verb set where the semantic exists: list / show / add / edit / remove / run / verify (+ noun-specific verbs only when unavoidable, e.g. history repair|redact|compact, plan materialize). Rationale: agents transfer knowledge across nouns without reading per-noun help (pm comments add == pm files add == pm schema add); wrong-verb retries drop the same way wrong-command retries do. Suggested test: a contracts-table lint asserting each noun's verbs are drawn from the shared vocabulary or an ADR-listed exception. Also suggest the ADR records a measurable acceptance target from the pm-a22j baseline (e.g. top-level help and contracts --summary each under a fixed token budget) so \"simpler\" is verifiable, not aesthetic."
  "2026-07-13T21:41:30.466Z",codex-root,"Decision support 2026-07-13: the current public root exposes 67 subcommands even though several are aliases, lifecycle variants, extension-owned audits, or an internal worker. The proposed noun-oriented grammar can reduce discovery burden without capability loss if compatibility aliases remain contract-visible, deprecation is measurable, extension ownership stays explicit, and JSON/TOON plus stdin/stdout composition remain first-class. Resolve this ADR before pm-0z7n and its downstream consolidation work; this audit records evidence only and does not accept the proposed decision."
  "2026-07-29T16:58:32.070Z","harness:claude-code","Placement input for the seven spellings that landed after the noun table (workspace, eval, item, duplicates, start-task, pause-task, close-task) plus a three-pattern classification of the residual redundancy and a fourth metric - distinct spelling-and-concept pairs rather than flag count - are recorded as a note on pm-n7rr dated 2026-07-29. The table, the fourteen bare-verb aliases and the five open rulings are unchanged; ruling C on package-contributed spellings and the new ruling this raises on whether item or the bare verbs are canonical are the two that gate the fold."
  "2026-07-31T14:18:44.365Z","harness:claude-code","Decision input 2026-07-31: the cost of leaving this proposed is now measurable, and one addition to the decision content is proposed.\n\nMeasured today: pm --help renders 87 unique top-level spellings and pm contracts --summary reports 71 canonical summaries, against roughly 60 when the consolidation epic opened on 2026-07-05 and 75 at the 07-25 census. That is about one new spelling per day and a 45 percent larger surface, accumulated entirely while this decision sat in proposed state. Every downstream consolidation child is sequenced behind acceptance, so the delay does not pause the problem - it lets it grow while the remedy waits.\n\nThe reason growth is frictionless is worth naming in the decision itself, because it is a governance gap rather than a discipline gap. scripts/measure-agent-token-surface.mjs --check does fail the first time a new top-level command appears, since compareBaseline treats an absent baseline entry as a violation. The documented remedy is to regenerate the baseline. So the surface has a ratchet that records growth and no budget that resists it: required_commands is now a 67-entry list of every command ever accepted, the gate passed green across 91 surfaces today, and nothing in that path consults this decision or checks grammar conformance.\n\nProposed addition to the accepted content, as one more principle alongside the shared verb vocabulary and scope-precedes-verb rules already commented here: a recorded ceiling on default-visible top-level spellings, owned by this decision and lowered as each family migrates. The ceiling makes the fence enforce the grammar rather than the byte count, and it turns each new name into an amendment to this ADR instead of a script rerun. It also gives the consolidation children a shared definition of progress - the ceiling falling - that does not depend on any single family landing first.\n\nSequencing input, argued in full in the epic note recorded today: the alias substrate pm-0z7n is decision-independent, because a table mapping retired spellings to canonical invocations plus migration hints does not require agreement on what the canonical spellings are. Landing it before acceptance rather than after would let this decision choose table contents instead of gating all downstream work, and would make each family migration reversible. That does not change the recommended grammar in any way; it changes only what has to be true before the first family can move."
notes[15]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-11T02:52:36.847Z",maintainer-agent,"Decision-support input 2026-07-11 (review pass): (1) Surface growth continues while proposed — top-level help now lists 64 entries (ADR context said ~60 on 07-06); contract table carries 105 commands + 86 actions. Every pass this stays open, the shim/parity work in pm-0z7n gets bigger. (2) NEW evidence the grammar must cover EXTENSION command synthesis: GH-503 (pm-s9iu) shows each extension group today spawns flattened top-level aliases (csv, csv-export, csv-import, jira, jira-sync all advertised in pm --help) and those aliases carry broken contracts (empty --help, no unknown-option validation). Recommend an explicit decision point: extensions contribute exactly ONE noun (the group); flattened <group>-<sub> aliases become hidden shims under point-5 policy or are not advertised in top-level help. That shrinks both the token surface and the GH-503 defect class at the design level. (3) Sequencing: points 1-4+6 are accept-able now — the only carried risk is the shim layer, and pm-0z7n already owns spelling-parity testing; this ADR gates pm-0z7n which gates 8 further items (tnud/tqel/pfqi/ik19/6apl/kcs4/yql1/o3fh/eq4x/0mox), the single largest decision bottleneck in the graph alongside pm-bl8x."
  "2026-07-11T03:06:46.059Z",maintainer-agent,"Decision-support input (pass evidence 2026-07-11, source GH-509/pm-fjxm): composed operations are a live grammar gap. GH-509 requests atomic select-and-claim and proposes the spelling `pm next --claim`, while main already ships `pm claim --next` (pm-114v). Two verbs each claiming the composition = exactly the surface-growth pattern this ADR exists to stop. Proposed rule for the ADR: (1) a composition lives on the MUTATING verb (claim), never the read verb (next) — reads stay side-effect-free so agents can poll them safely; (2) when a mutating verb embeds a selector, it reuses the read verb's filter contract verbatim (one shared candidate-selector flag set, defined once in cli-contracts), never a re-spelled subset. Consequence: GH-509 lands as claim --next + next's filters, and `next --claim` is rejected as a spelling. This also generalizes: any future `pm <read> --<mutate>` request should be flipped to `pm <mutate> --<selector>`."
  "2026-07-26T10:50:06.570Z","harness:claude-code","Grammar input 2026-07-26: five core nouns need an ownership ruling under the noun-first principle — graph, merge, events, copy, normalize. graph is the substantive one: it is a 16-subcommand analytics and governance family, and the plausible readings are that it is its own domain noun, that its query verbs belong under a read noun while its audit and index verbs belong under ops, or that it is the read surface of the relationship facet already enumerated under item facets. merge and events are single-verb surfaces that plausibly fold under history or ops. copy is a create variant. normalize is a governance verb that already exists as a package capability. The earlier ruling pattern on this ADR — restore under history because it replays audited state, eval under ops because it is diagnostic — is the precedent to apply."
  "2026-07-26T22:10:51.661Z","harness:claude-code","Decision-forcing evidence, measured 2026-07-27: the surface this ADR proposes to consolidate is growing faster than the ADR is being decided.\n\nTop-level spelling count taken from the shipped build at each census recorded on pm-n7rr, plus today's:\n\n  2026-07-05  ~60   (the count this ADR was written against)\n  2026-07-06   64\n  2026-07-07   65\n  2026-07-18   73\n  2026-07-25   77\n  2026-07-26   88\n  2026-07-27   89\n\nThat is +29 spellings in 22 days, roughly +1.3 per day, or +48 percent against the number in the ADR text. The proposal itself has not changed and is not the bottleneck; the twelve-noun table, the shared verb vocabulary and the placement disposition for the five previously unplaced nouns are all recorded on pm-n7rr as notes 8, 11 and 13, and every option there is alias-preserving with zero feature loss.\n\nTwo consequences follow for whoever decides this.\n\nFirst, the cost of deferral is not zero and is not constant. Every new top-level spelling added while the ADR sits open is another alias the shim layer must carry forever under the alias-preserving policy this ADR itself mandates. Deferral does not preserve optionality here, it spends it: the migration gets strictly larger and the eventual v1.0 grammar freeze on pm-xp1xsw freezes a larger surface.\n\nSecond, an interim control is available that does not require deciding the ADR. Accepting or rejecting the noun table is a design judgment; refusing to let the count grow while that judgment is pending is not. That control has been recorded as an acceptance criterion on pm-wt43zj so it can ship independently of this decision, and it should be read as reducing the pressure on this ADR rather than pre-empting it.\n\nNothing here argues for a particular disposition of the noun table. It argues that the option being exercised by not deciding — a surface that keeps growing under an alias-preservation promise — is the one option nobody chose."
  "2026-07-28T22:34:29.952Z","harness:claude-code","Complete placement table, recorded 2026-07-28. This is the artifact the ADR has been missing: every rendered top-level spelling mapped to exactly one target noun-verb path, so acceptance is a ruling on a table rather than on a principle. It supersedes nothing and renumbers nothing; the four earlier decision-support notes stay as recorded. Live census at HEAD: 85 rendered top-level spellings, 70 contracted command summaries.\n\nTWELVE NOUNS. item, list, context, search, graph, history, workspace, package, ops, plan, contracts, help.\n\nThe tension this table resolves. Noun-first consolidation is right for legibility and wrong for the hot path: an agent that runs create, get, update, close, claim, release hundreds of times per session should not pay two tokens where one suffices, and the recorded op histogram says thirteen operations are 99.4 percent of all mutations with the annotation family alone at 46.7 percent. So the rule proposed here is asymmetric on purpose. Canonical form is always noun-verb. A named set of fourteen bare-verb spellings on the hottest paths are PERMANENT aliases, declared as such in contract data, exempt from deprecation and counted separately from the legacy hidden aliases that exist only for compatibility. Everything else consolidates.\n\nNOUN 1 - item. The unit of work, its lifecycle, and its facets.\n  create           -> item create              permanent bare alias\n  copy             -> item create --from       hidden alias\n  get              -> item get                 permanent bare alias\n  update           -> item update              permanent bare alias\n  update-many      -> item update --many       hidden alias\n  close            -> item close               permanent bare alias\n  close-many       -> item close --many        hidden alias\n  delete           -> item delete              permanent bare alias\n  restore          -> item restore             permanent bare alias, see open ruling A\n  claim            -> item claim               permanent bare alias\n  release          -> item release             permanent bare alias\n  append           -> item append              hidden alias\n  start-task       -> item claim --start       hidden alias\n  pause-task       -> item release --pause     hidden alias\n  close-task       -> item close               hidden alias\n  comments         -> item comments            permanent bare alias\n  notes            -> item notes               permanent bare alias\n  learnings        -> item learnings           permanent bare alias\n  files            -> item files               permanent bare alias\n  docs             -> item docs                permanent bare alias\n  deps             -> item deps                permanent bare alias, see open ruling D\n  test             -> item test                see open ruling B\n  item             -> the noun itself, already rendered\n  meet, event, remind -> calendar package create sugar, leaves core, owned by pm-o3fh\n\nNOUN 2 - list. Everything that returns rows selected by predicate.\n  list             -> list\n  list-all, list-draft, list-open, list-in-progress, list-blocked, list-closed, list-canceled\n                   -> list --status <s>        seven hidden aliases, owned by pm-pfqi\n  aggregate        -> list --group-by          hidden alias, owned by pm-xkgq\n\nNOUN 3 - context. The working set and what to do next.\n  context, ctx     -> context\n  next             -> context next             permanent bare alias, owned by pm-kcs4\n  focus            -> context focus            hidden alias, owned by pm-kcs4\n\nNOUN 4 - search. Retrieval by content.\n  search           -> search\n  search-advanced  -> package-contributed, renders under its owning package, see open ruling C\n\nNOUN 5 - graph. Workspace-scoped traversal and analytics over typed edges. Promoted to a canonical noun per the 2026-07-26 placement note.\n  graph            -> graph\n\nNOUN 6 - history. The immutable record and its maintenance.\n  history          -> history\n  events           -> history events\n  activity         -> history activity\n  history-redact   -> history redact\n  history-repair   -> history repair\n  history-compact  -> history compact\n  history-author-acknowledge -> history author-acknowledge\n  All four history-* consolidations are owned by pm-tqel.\n\nNOUN 7 - workspace. The tracker as an object: how it is created, configured, shaped, snapshotted, and reconciled across branches.\n  init             -> workspace init           permanent bare alias\n  config           -> workspace config         permanent bare alias\n  schema           -> workspace schema\n  profile          -> workspace profile\n  templates        -> workspace templates      package-contributed\n  merge            -> workspace merge          machine-facing git integration, absent from the agent-facing table\n  workspace        -> the noun itself, already rendered, see open ruling E\n\nNOUN 8 - package. Extensibility lifecycle. Owned by pm-tnud.\n  package, packages -> package\n  extension        -> package                  hidden alias\n  install          -> package install          hidden alias\n  upgrade          -> package upgrade          hidden alias\n\nNOUN 9 - ops. Diagnostics and maintenance that act on the workspace rather than an item. Owned by pm-6apl.\n  stats            -> ops stats\n  health           -> ops health\n  validate         -> ops validate\n  gc               -> ops gc\n  telemetry        -> ops telemetry\n  eval             -> ops eval\n  normalize        -> ops normalize            placement resolved here per the 2026-07-26 note, with the validate --auto-fix overlap dispositioned at the same time\n  reindex          -> ops reindex\n  duplicates       -> ops duplicates\n  test-all         -> ops test                 see open ruling B\n\nNOUN 10 - plan. Durable multi-step planning.\n  plan             -> plan\n\nNOUN 11 - contracts. The machine-readable surface description. Stays a top-level noun because it is the bootstrap path every other surface is discovered through, and burying it under help would make discovery depend on the human surface.\n  contracts        -> contracts\n\nNOUN 12 - help. The human twin of contracts.\n  help             -> help\n  pm               -> declared exempt self-name, see pm-1jrdri\n\nPACKAGE-CONTRIBUTED SPELLINGS. Sixteen of the eighty-five come from packages and render identically to core commands, which is the single largest legibility problem in the current surface and is not solved by any noun placement: beads, cal, calendar, changelog, command-kit, comments-audit, dedupe-audit, dedupe-merge, duplicates, eval, normalize, reindex, search-advanced, templates, test-runs, todos, twin, vcs. An agent cannot tell from help which of these exist in a bare install, and pm-n7rr already records that they exit 2 when the owning package is absent. The table above places each by function, but the ADR must also rule on whether package-contributed commands render in a distinct section, under a package-qualified path, or with a declared owner field that help renders.\n\nFIVE OPEN RULINGS the ADR must settle to be acceptable.\n\nRuling A - restore. This table places restore under item because it restores one item to one version. pm-tqel proposes folding restore under history alongside redact, repair and compact. Both readings are defensible: the mechanism is a history replay, the subject is an item. Proposed resolution: item restore is canonical because the noun is the subject not the mechanism, and history keeps stream-level maintenance only. This also keeps the pm-34w8za consolidation coherent, since that item groups redact, repair and compact as stream maintenance and explicitly reaches into restore only as a clone source.\n\nRuling B - test has three scopes and one spelling. item test manages and runs the linked tests of one item. ops test runs everything. test-runs inspects the package-owned background runner. Proposed resolution: item test and ops test as above, test-runs stays package-contributed, and the internal test-runs-worker entrypoint is hidden per pm-lp4j.\n\nRuling C - package-contributed rendering, as described above.\n\nRuling D - deps versus graph. Both read the same typed edges. Proposed resolution: item deps is the item-scoped, budget-bounded relationship view an agent uses inside a task; graph is the workspace-scoped analytic surface. They are different questions over one substrate and both keep their names, with the boundary declared in contract data so neither grows into the other.\n\nRuling E - the workspace noun is already occupied and uncontracted. workspace renders in help today and exits 2 on pm contracts --command workspace, filed as pm-1jrdri. Adopting workspace as a canonical noun means that defect must be fixed first, otherwise the grammar's own noun is the one command the contract system cannot describe.\n\nWHAT THIS TABLE DOES NOT DO. It removes nothing. Every one of the eighty-five spellings resolves after consolidation, fourteen as permanent bare aliases and the remainder as hidden compatibility aliases counted by pm-0mox. It does not renumber or supersede the existing consolidation tasks under pm-n7rr; each task keeps its scope and gains a target path from this table. And it does not decide the flag lexicon: pm-fs8q9x must land first, because folding the seven list-* variants into list --status is a null move for token cost if the flag spelling per concept is still ambiguous."
  "2026-07-29T04:47:09.463Z","harness:claude-code","Ruling F, proposed 2026-07-29: the unit of consolidation is the flag concept, not the command noun, and the noun table is the second tranche rather than the first.\n\nThe measurement that forces it. The live surface is 84 commands, 1,792 flag occurrences and 481 distinct flag spellings. Two hundred spellings appear on exactly one command. The eight list-family commands carry 63 to 65 flags each and differ in one status literal. Create, update and update-many together spend 288 occurrences, sixteen percent of the whole surface, re-declaring one item schema three times.\n\nWhat the noun table does and does not buy. Collapsing 85 spellings to 12 nouns with 14 permanent bare-verb aliases makes the index legible and makes the product describable in one screen, which is worth doing and stays the target shape. It removes zero flag spellings. An agent that has learned the twelve nouns still cannot predict whether a filter it used on list works on aggregate, still cannot predict whether an acceptance criterion is set with --ac or --add-ac, and still faces 200 spellings it can only learn by reading one command's help. The noun table changes what the agent reads first; the flag lexicon changes how much it has to remember.\n\nThe ruling. Sequence the program as flag-lexicon first, nouns second. Concretely: a declared filter predicate consumed by every query surface, a declared item field set consumed by every mutation verb, and a declared disposition of local or shared on every one of the 200 singleton spellings. Only then re-render the nouns, because at that point the noun table is a presentation change over a governed vocabulary rather than a rename over an ungoverned one.\n\nConsequence for rulings A through E. None of them is invalidated, but three change character. Ruling B, the three scopes of test, is a field-set question once the field set is declared, because the scopes differ in which declared fields apply rather than in which verb is spelled. Ruling D, deps versus graph, is a predicate question once the predicate is declared, because the two commands differ in traversal semantics and not in filtering. Ruling C, the sixteen package-contributed spellings, becomes tractable only after the singleton disposition exists, because a package flag is by definition a singleton and today nothing distinguishes a package flag from a core one in the rendered surface. Rulings A and E are unaffected.\n\nFalsifiable prediction attached to this ruling. If the noun consolidation lands before the flag work, the count of distinct flag spellings will not fall and the per-command help total will not fall by more than the removed index rows, roughly 3 percent of 212,496 bytes. That is checkable after the fact and should be checked, because if it turns out false this ruling was wrong."
  "2026-07-30T09:25:09.376Z","harness:claude-code","Ruling F (open) — one flag name carries exactly one value domain across the whole surface, and a paired flag shares one value grammar with its partner\n\nTwo live violations force this ruling rather than leaving it implicit in the noun table.\n\nFirst, --for. It resolves a declared read intent on get, list, next and search, and it is validated against the context section enum on context. The intent applier assigns a contract's included_field_groups array straight into the section option, and four of the five groups in one declared intent are not section names, so both context intents are unreachable and the resulting error names --section, a flag the caller never passed. The grammar question this raises is not a bug report: it is whether a flag name is allowed to mean different things on different nouns at all. Ruling: no. A flag name is a surface-wide term. If two commands need different domains, they need different flag names, or one contract-declared mapping between the domains that is itself testable.\n\nSecond, --dep and --dep-remove. The add half refuses the unprefixed comma form with an explicit message naming both accepted shapes. The remove half accepts it, matches nothing, and exits 0 reporting zero changed fields. Ruling: a mutating flag pair over one concept declares one value grammar, and both halves validate against it. A remove that matches nothing is a diagnosable outcome, not success.\n\nConsequence for the twelve-noun table. Both rulings are preconditions for the table being writable as data rather than prose. A noun table that fixes command names while flag names remain per-command terms has not fixed the grammar - it has only fixed the first token. Ruling F therefore belongs in the same ADR as the noun placement and should be settled before the consolidation slices begin, because every slice that moves a spelling also moves its flags.\n\nRelationship to the existing open rulings. A (restore placement), B (test's three scopes), C (the 16 package-contributed spellings) and D (deps versus graph) all decide where a capability lives. E decides whether an uncontracted noun may render. F is the first ruling about the token after the noun, which is why it does not collapse into any of them."
  "2026-07-30T17:12:44.179Z","harness:claude-code","Ruling G (open), proposed 2026-07-30: a surface may not declare a bound it cannot satisfy, and an all-or-nothing degradation is not a degradation\n\nRulings A through F stand unchanged. Ruling G is the grammar consequence of what enforcement of declared output budgets exposed on the live tracker this afternoon.\n\nObserved. Three of five built-in read intents declare a token ceiling below the smallest projection their own command can emit, so the declared bound is unreachable and the response carries no result. The receipt reports being within budget in the same envelope in which it reports having omitted the result, because the measurement is taken after the payload was deleted. The documented recovery is to re-run without the projection, which emits more tokens than the projection would have. And the flag that could raise the ceiling is absent from three of the five commands that declare one.\n\nRuling, three clauses.\n\nFirst, satisfiability. A command may declare a bound only if the leanest form it can render fits inside it, and the build must check that against a corpus at least the size of the project's own tracker rather than against a fixture. A declaration that cannot be met is not a contract, it is a permanently failing assertion pointed at the caller.\n\nSecond, graded degradation. Where a bound can be exceeded, the grammar must express at least one intermediate response between the full form and nothing: fewer rows, a leaner tier, or a stated sample. A path whose only tier below full output is an empty receipt has not degraded, it has failed, and it should say so with an error code rather than with a success envelope carrying a truthful-looking receipt.\n\nThird, override co-location. Wherever a bound is declared, the flag that adjusts it is declared on the same command with the same name and the same value domain, which is Ruling F applied to this pair. A ceiling an agent cannot raise on the surface that imposed it is a dead end, and the omission is invisible until the ceiling is hit.\n\nCorollary for receipts. A receipt reports on what was emitted. Where nothing was emitted, the within-budget field is not true, it is not applicable, and the envelope must distinguish an infeasible declaration from an exceeded one so a caller can tell a configuration error from a large answer.\n\nScope. Ruling G governs the grammar of bounded output. It does not choose the projection tiers, which is a context-engine algorithm and is catalogued separately, and it does not set the numbers, which belongs to the output-budget contract. It rules only that a declared number must be reachable, that there must be a rung between everything and nothing, and that the adjustment lives where the declaration lives."
  "2026-07-31T06:13:11.805Z","harness:claude-code","Ruling H — output bounding is a shared dimension set inherited by every read noun, not a per-command flag choice; and it is sequenced before any noun is moved\n\nPrior rulings stand. This one resolves the placement question that two independently recorded simplification axes converged on, and it fixes the ordering of the consolidation programme.\n\nThe measurement it rules on. Eighteen distinct output-bounding flag spellings exist across nineteen read commands at HEAD, no two commands expose the same set, four read surfaces expose none at all, and the widest pairwise availability disagreement is ten. The one flag that can raise a token ceiling is absent from all three surfaces that enforce declared ceilings. Four near-synonyms coexist on one command with no way to infer from their names which produces least output.\n\nRuling H.1 — dimensions, not flags. The grammar declares exactly four output-bounding dimensions: what to include, how much of it, what it may cost, and how it is encoded. A read command does not choose its bounding flags. It inherits the dimension set, and the contract table records per command whether each dimension is supported, and if not, why. Adding a new bounding flag to one command is a grammar change requiring an amendment to this decision, not a command-local decision.\n\nRuling H.2 — inapplicable is not unknown. Where a surface genuinely cannot support a dimension, supplying it is accepted and reported as inapplicable in the envelope, with the reason from the contract table. It is never rejected as an unknown option. An unknown-option error for a dimension the grammar declares universal teaches an agent that the grammar is not universal, which is the precise cost this epic exists to remove.\n\nRuling H.3 — a declared precedence algebra. When two dimensions are supplied and they disagree, the contract states which wins, and the envelope states which was applied. Discovering the answer by experiment — as is currently required when a depth flag and an intent flag are combined — is not an acceptable outcome for a surface an agent calls on every invocation.\n\nRuling H.4 — aliases are permanent and lossless. All eighteen current spellings survive as hidden aliases with one-line migration hints, consistent with the alias-preservation policy this ADR already fixes. No recorded invocation may break. The alias-usage counters decide removal eligibility, and removal remains out of scope until the general-availability grammar freeze.\n\nRuling H.5 — sequencing. The bounding dimension set lands before any noun is moved. Two axes reached this independently: folding the status-variant list commands into one noun is a null move while the flag lexicon is inconsistent, and folding nouns is a null move for an agent that must still learn a per-command availability matrix to ask for less output. The bounding set is the smallest self-contained tranche in the programme and the only one an agent pays for on every single call. The noun-consolidation slices are recorded as blocked by it in the graph, so this ruling is traversable rather than prose.\n\nRuling H.6 — the metric is disagreement, not count. Acceptance is measured as the pairwise availability disagreement between any two read surfaces over the dimension set, currently ten, and the number of read surfaces supporting zero dimensions, currently four. Target zero and zero. Both are computed from the contract table, so this is a contract test and not a review. A total-flag count is explicitly rejected as the metric because it can be improved by hiding spellings, which changes nothing an agent experiences.\n\nRuling H.7 — self-descriptions are executable. Any instruction the product publishes about how to bound or extract output — a jq selector in a row contract, a restore-with hint in an omission receipt, a recovery bundle naming commands that accept a flag — must be executable and correct when executed, asserted by a gate. A published instruction that returns nothing is worse than no instruction, because it fails silently and indistinguishably from a legitimate empty result. This ruling extends the contracts-are-the-source-of-truth principle from generation to verification: it is not enough that surfaces are generated from the contract table; the instructions those surfaces emit must be checked against the envelopes that carry them."
  "2026-08-01T13:07:54.517Z","harness:claude-code","Ruling I, recorded 2026-08-01. Rulings A through H stand and none is reopened.\n\nTwo clauses, both grounded in measurements taken today against 2026.8.1.\n\nClause one, on value domains. A flag whose legal values form a closed set known at the failure site must render that set in the refusal, complete and untruncated, and must render it in its own help text. A worked example may accompany the domain and may never substitute for it. In particular a completeness word such as valid, allowed, or must be one of may never introduce a proper subset. The live counter-example is the field projection selector, whose help reads \"valid: --fields id,title\" for a domain of at least twenty-four members, and whose refusal names the rejected value and no legal one. Thirteen of the fifteen domains probed today already satisfy this clause, so it codifies the majority behavior rather than inventing one, and it makes the two exceptions defects against a written rule instead of matters of taste.\n\nClause two, on the executable position. A leading positional token that exactly matches one of this package's own declared binary names is the invocation of this tool, never a noun in this tool's grammar. It must be absorbed before subcommand resolution. The grammar therefore reserves those names and refuses to allocate them as command nouns. This is not a convenience: package runners for both npm and bun forward the binary name into the argument vector rather than dispatching on it, so under the grammar as written today the two most natural published invocations of this tool are both refused by this tool, while a version probe against the identical broken form exits zero. The rule also disposes of the long-standing anomaly that a token named pm is enumerated in root help and resolves to nothing, which two prior items recorded as an enumeration defect without a grammar rule to appeal to.\n\nConsequence for the noun table. The reserved-name clause removes one entry from the candidate noun space rather than adding one, so it composes with consolidation instead of competing with it. The value-domain clause adds no nouns and no verbs at all; it constrains what a refusal must contain, which is the surface an agent meets most often and the one the noun table never described."
  "2026-08-02T15:04:52.323Z","harness:claude-code","Ruling J (proposed, 2026-08-02) — a rendered notation has one meaning surface-wide, and a command may not declare an arity it does not enforce.\n\nContext. Rulings A-I fixed noun placement, verb vocabulary, completeness words, flag value domains, and bounding declarations. All of them govern names and values. None governs the notation the help renderer uses to describe argument SHAPE, and that notation is currently ambiguous.\n\nEvidence at 2026.8.2: square brackets mean genuinely omittable on telemetry, item, workspace and config (exit 0 with the slot absent) and mean refused-with-exit-2 on schema, profile and plan. Angle brackets mean required on graph and merge, and those two produce 'Missing required argument subcommand' while the three false-optionals produce 'Invalid argument value' for the identical condition.\n\nRuling. (1) Optional notation is a promise the runtime must keep: if omitting a slot is refused, the slot is required and must render as required. (2) One condition, one refusal class: a missing required positional produces the missing-required-argument code on every command, never the invalid-value code, which is reserved for a value that was supplied and rejected. (3) A positional slot's name is part of the contract, so two commands accepting the same concept accept it in the same slot position with the same arity - the item id may not be required on 13 commands and optional on 4 without a declared rule that predicts which. (4) A polymorphic slot, where the meaning of one position depends on whether another is filled, must be declared as polymorphic in the contracts table and must carry a refusal that names both accepted forms; pm create already meets this bar and is the reference implementation - 'pm create Task' refuses by naming both 'pm create Task \"<title>\"' and 'pm create \"<title>\" --type Task' and explaining that the guard exists to prevent silently creating a Task titled Task. (5) Consolidation may not reduce the noun count while leaving the shape count untouched: the target-surface table declares a positional signature per noun.\n\nEnforcement vehicle: pm-ya7x55 (positional lexicon and conformance gate), gated behind pm-wt43zj's contracts table. Negative controls for the gate are schema, profile and plan as they stand today."
  "2026-08-02T21:15:32.982Z","harness:claude-code","Ruling K, recorded 2026-08-02: a verb may not be documented as 'run this after X' when the tool can detect X.\n\nRulings A through J stand unchanged; none is restated. Ruling K governs the class of commands whose correctness is conditional on repository state rather than on their own arguments.\n\nStatement. If a command is only correct at a particular point in a workflow, then either the tool detects that point and says so, or the condition is part of the command's declared contract and its refusal names it. Prose guidance that the caller must remember is not an acceptable third option. Concretely: a command that must be run once per clone must report, from any invocation in an unprepared clone, that it has not been run; a command that is meaningful only while some state is pending must refuse or no-op with that state named; and a command documented as a follow-up to another operation must be able to tell the caller whether that operation has occurred.\n\nRationale, consistent with ruling I (a completeness word may never introduce a proper subset) and ruling F (every surface answers the same question the same way). An agent's cost is not the command list, it is the number of facts it must hold that pm could have told it. A conditional verb whose condition is undetectable converts a one-command interaction into a remembered protocol, and a remembered protocol is exactly what a fresh session does not have.\n\nReference implementation to cite rather than invent: pm create's guard on a leading positional that names a type, which refuses with the condition stated instead of accepting and doing something else (ruling J). The counter-example, and the reason this ruling is being written now: the post-merge family, where six surfaces share one condition, the condition is fully derivable from recorded receipts and drift state, and it is nonetheless carried in transient driver stdout and in a sentence in AGENTS.md. See the surface axis note on pm-n7rr for the enumeration and pm-lwmstb for the receipt half.\n\nConsequence for the grammar freeze: the noun-verb table must record, per verb, whether it is unconditional or state-conditional, and a state-conditional verb without a detectable condition cannot pass the conformance gate."
  "2026-08-03T06:28:45.547Z","harness:claude-code","Ruling L, recorded 2026-08-03: a read may not apply a filter that its own receipt does not name.\n\nRulings A through K stand unchanged; none is restated. Ruling L governs defaults on the read side of the grammar, as ruling K governs preconditions on the write side.\n\nStatement. Every filter a read applies is either supplied by the caller or disclosed in the envelope, spelled exactly as the flag that would reproduce it. A read may have opinionated defaults; it may not have invisible ones. Two consequences follow and both are binding. First, the receipt must round-trip: re-invoking the command with precisely the filters the envelope reports must return precisely the rows it returned. Second, no completeness field may describe a defaulted subset as the whole: a count named total must be the count before defaulting, or it must be renamed, and a flag named has_more may report false only when nothing was withheld for any reason, defaulting included.\n\nRationale, and why this is a grammar ruling rather than a bug report. The project already accepted that field-level omission must be receipted, which is why omission_receipt exists and names the flag that restores what was dropped. Row-level omission by default has no equivalent, and the fields that would carry the signal deny it instead. Measured on 2026-08-03: pm list --type Issue reports count 127, total 127, has_more false, and a filters block naming only type and limit, in a workspace holding 722 items of that type. The same corpus through pm search is unscoped, so the two discovery surfaces answer 'what exists' differently and neither says which it did. That is ruling F's failure mode - surfaces disagreeing about one question - reached through a default rather than through a divergent implementation.\n\nThe cost is not the hidden rows. It is that a receipt which under-reports its own filtering cannot be composed. The project promises that pm composes with grep, jq and xargs; a jq expression is downstream of the envelope and has no standing to doubt a total the envelope asserts. An undisclosed default is therefore not a preference, it is a defect in the contract that makes shell composition sound.\n\nRelation to the other rulings. Ruling I forbade a completeness word from naming a proper subset in prose; ruling L forbids the same thing in structured output, where it is worse, because prose is read by a human who can be sceptical and an envelope field is read by a program that cannot. Ruling K required a state-conditional verb to detect its own condition; ruling L requires a defaulted read to declare its own scope. Both replace something the caller must remember with something the tool must say.\n\nConsequence for the grammar freeze. The noun-verb table records, per read, its default scope as an explicit entry rather than as an absence, and the conformance gate asserts filter round-trip for every row-producing read. A read whose disclosed filters do not reproduce its own rows cannot pass. See pm-999jh7 for the measurement and pm-sb0tns for the shared row contract this rides on."
  "2026-08-03T20:40:23.745Z","harness:claude-code","Ruling M — lifecycle grammar symmetry.\n\nExtends the ruling ledger. Rulings A through L stand unchanged and none is restated.\n\nStatement. Any input the opening verb of a lifecycle accepts, the closing verb of that lifecycle accepts, under the same flag name and the same value grammar. Where an asymmetry is deliberate, it is recorded as a decision with the reason, and the refusal at the asymmetric end names that decision rather than reporting an unknown option.\n\nEvidence that produced the ruling. Measured 2026-08-03: create accepts nine inline evidence flags covering acceptance criteria, comment, dependency, doc, file, learning, note and test. close accepts ten flags and not one of them carries evidence. The lifecycle is therefore composable at the opening and decomposed at the closing, and the prescribed completion protocol is seven invocations where the prescribed creation is one. Nothing in the contract states this asymmetry, no decision records it, and the refusal at the closing end is an ordinary unknown-option error indistinguishable from a typo.\n\nWhy it belongs in the noun-verb ruling set rather than in a defect. The defect is filed separately and is about cost and atomicity. The ruling is about what the grammar is allowed to look like. Without it, the natural repair is to add evidence flags to close under whatever names are convenient, which grows the surface by nine more spellings and reintroduces the class this decision exists to prevent. With it, the repair is constrained in advance: the names already exist, so nothing new is spelled, and the only open question is whether the composed form is a flag set on close or a distinct verb, which is a placement question the noun table already knows how to answer.\n\nScope and non-scope. The ruling governs the pairing of verbs across one lifecycle, not the total flag count of any verb. It does not require that every verb accept every flag. It does not apply between unrelated verbs. It applies to the item lifecycle first and to any future lifecycle the schema introduces, including custom statuses with terminal roles, which is what makes it a rule rather than a patch to one command.\n\nConsequence for the current ledger. Ruling F already forbids one flag name from having two value domains across the surface. Ruling J already establishes that optional notation is a promise. M completes the pair on the axis of lifecycle position: F governs a name across commands, J governs a name against its own declaration, M governs a name across the beginning and end of the thing it describes."
  "2026-08-04T05:54:30.059Z","harness:claude-code","Ruling N — acceptance implies reachability.\n\nExtends the ruling ledger. Rulings A through M stand unchanged and none is restated.\n\nStatement. Any value the write path accepts into a closed domain must be resolvable by every consumer declared to read that domain. Where a consumer cannot resolve an accepted value, the write path must canonicalize it at write time into a spelling that consumer does resolve. A vocabulary is not defined by what the store will hold. It is defined by what the readers will resolve, and any difference between the two is a silent data-loss channel rather than a tolerance.\n\nWhy this is a grammar ruling and not a defect note. Ruling M fixed symmetry between the two verbs of one lifecycle, so that an input the opening verb accepts the closing verb also accepts. Ruling N fixes the orthogonal asymmetry, between the writer and the reader of a single value. M is about two commands agreeing on a grammar. N is about one grammar being honoured by everything downstream of it.\n\nThe evidence this session is the dependency-kind domain, which fails the ruling twice over. The domain accepts fifteen spellings for roughly eight relations. The kind blocks is accepted, stored verbatim, indexed, counted in the graph profile, and correctly resolved by graph successors and graph predecessors, and it is invisible to the readiness resolver that pm next, pm context, and pm list-blocked all share. The kind related_to is accepted and stored verbatim and is folded into related only at read time by the graph registry, so a jq or grep pipeline over the store, which the project documents as a first-class way to use pm, selects on the wrong token and silently returns a short answer. Exactly one alias in the whole domain, depends_on, is canonicalized on write, which proves the mechanism exists and was simply never generalized.\n\nThe failure mode is worth naming precisely because it is invisible from both ends. The write succeeds and returns success. The value persists and survives round-trip. The consumer behaves as though the value had never been written. Nothing in the recorded history distinguishes the accepted-but-unreachable write from a write that was never attempted, so the defect cannot be found by reading the record. It can only be found by writing the same fact in two spellings and diffing the consumers, which is the shape of probe this ledger keeps rediscovering.\n\nScope of the ruling beyond dependency kinds. The same test applies to every closed value domain the grammar declares: statuses that a workflow accepts but a transition table does not name, types that create accepts but a projection does not render, intents that a budget declares but a selector does not branch on, and flag aliases that parse but do not reach the contract table. Each is one accepted value plus one blind consumer.\n\nThe conformance obligation this creates. For every closed domain, the accepted set is enumerable from the contracts surface, and the declared consumers of that domain are enumerable too. A conformance test takes the cross product and asserts that each accepted value is resolvable by each declared consumer, or that the write path rewrote it to one that is. A new spelling added without a consumer then fails the build rather than entering the store. This is the general form of the check pm-jkbqt8 already requires for one domain and pm-4020c5 for another, stated once so the next domain does not have to rediscover it.\n\nConsequence for the consolidation this ADR governs. The alias-preserving deprecation policy of ruling five in the decision body is safe precisely and only under this ruling. Preserving every historical spelling is a promise that the old spelling still works, and a spelling that parses but does not reach the feature is not a preserved spelling. It is a removal that reports success. Consolidation without ruling N would convert a visible breaking change into an invisible one, which is worse."
body: "## Context\npm has ~60 top-level commands. Growth pattern: each feature added a new top-level entry (8 list-* status variants, 4 history-* commands, extension+package+install+upgrade, start-task/pause-task/close-task, meet/event/remind). Agents pay for this in context tokens (help + contracts) and in wrong-command retries.\n\n## Decision (proposed)\n1. Noun-first grammar: ONE top-level command per domain noun; target ~15 nouns.\n2. Verbs/facets become subcommands (pm history repair) or flags (pm list --status closed), never new top-level commands.\n3. Projections (status/type/format) are flags.\n4. Sugar (shortcuts, lifecycle aliases, calendar) = hidden aliases or package-owned commands.\n5. Zero spelling removal: every current invocation keeps working via alias shims that emit a one-line migration hint (config-suppressible: ux.deprecation_hints=false).\n6. Contract table is the single source of truth; tiered help, completions, MCP tool names, and docs are generated from it.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n- Status quo (flat surface): rejected — surface keeps growing linearly with features.\n- Hard rename with removals: rejected — breaks agents/scripts; violates zero-feature-loss constraint.\n- Separate porcelain/plumbing binaries: rejected — doubles distribution surface; contradicts single-CLI ergonomics.\n\n## Consequences\n+ Help fits one screen; contracts shrink; discoverability via noun help pages.\n- Alias shim layer must be tested (spelling-parity spec) and carried for at least one deprecation cycle.\n- Full wiring checklist per rename (commands/index, register-mutation, mcp/server, sdk/cli-contracts, contracts.ts, completions bash/zsh/fish, docs, vitest.config, contracts:update)."
